    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chronic graft-versus-host disease (cGVHD) often arises following hematopoietic cell transplantation (HCT), impeding the success of the treatment. Previous studies have shown that in sex-mismatched HCT between a female donor and a male recipient, the donor immune system will develop antibodies against certain proteins encoded on the Y chromosome that associate with cGVHD, called HY antibodies. Detection of these alloantibodies, meaning antibodies that are produced by the donor immune system, has traditionally been performed via an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA). However, ELISA requires a large amount of patient serum, is inefficient for testing multiple antigens concurrently, and is unable to detect antibodies against short peptides. Protein microarrays have the potential to overcome these shortcomings. Here, we optimize the protein microarray method for HY antibody detection. Six microarray surfaces were tested for their ability to bind recombinant protein and peptide antigens. Using normal male donors as a baseline, we quantified HY antibody levels in male patients with female donors and compared these measurements to those previously gathered by ELISA. Overall, the epoxide-based surface manufactured by Schott maximized positive signal detection and produced the most reproducible results against both peptide and protein antigens. Significant levels of HY antibodies as measured by microarray were found to associate with cGVHD, suggesting the platform’s clinical utility in predicting cGHVD development. Moreover, detecting antibodies against short peptides with microarrays will allow for the study of monoclonal HY-targeting B cell populations, providing a detailed understanding of the mechanism behind cGVHD and allowing for enhanced prophylactic treatments.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
